1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,343
A developer says this on a Thursday. A
commit is a saved snapshot of the project, a

2
00:00:09,383 --> 00:00:14,168
pull request is a proposal on GitHub to
merge a branch of commits, and a push asks

3
00:00:14,208 --> 00:00:16,874
the server to move its
branch to your commits.

4
00:00:17,214 --> 00:00:21,621
The obvious reactions are all changes:
push with force, pull, rebase, or the

5
00:00:21,661 --> 00:00:26,302
classic, delete the clone and start again.
Any of them could destroy two days of

6
00:00:26,342 --> 00:00:29,755
work, because nobody yet knows
where those two commits are.

7
00:00:30,095 --> 00:00:34,576
Chapter 29 opens with the fact that shapes
this whole part of the course: most

8
00:00:34,616 --> 00:00:38,227
damage is done after the incident,
by the first repair attempt.

9
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:41,879
The original problem in a Git
repository is rarely destructive.

10
00:00:41,919 --> 00:00:46,399
The second command, typed before the
state was understood, is what removes the

11
00:00:46,439 --> 00:00:49,413
uncommitted change or rewrites
the published branch.

12
00:00:49,453 --> 00:00:53,818
So where are those two commits? Today
we find them without changing a thing.

13
00:00:55,658 --> 00:00:59,292
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:59,332 --> 00:01:02,845
This is Part 9, production
debugging and incident response.

15
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:07,844
Every earlier part gave you mechanisms.
This part gives you the method that selects

16
00:01:07,884 --> 00:01:11,638
the right one when all you have
is a sentence from a colleague.

17
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:17,070
You met the root-cause framework in video
4 and the ten-command diagnosis in video

18
00:01:17,110 --> 00:01:22,326
5, when you knew very little Git. Today you
apply the same framework end to end with

19
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:27,583
everything you've learned since. The case
turns on a mechanism from video 53: what a

20
00:01:27,623 --> 00:01:32,527
rebase does internally, where HEAD is
during it, and when the branch ref moves.

21
00:01:32,867 --> 00:01:36,170
A rebase copies a branch's
commits onto a new base.

22
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:41,677
HEAD is Git's note of where you are. And a
ref is a name that points at a commit, as

23
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:42,595
a branch does.

24
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:47,712
A CTO doesn't ask "which command fixes
this". The questions are: what happened, how

25
00:01:47,752 --> 00:01:52,472
do you know, what will the fix change, and
how do we stop it from happening again.

26
00:01:52,512 --> 00:01:54,852
The method is built to answer those four.

27
00:01:55,192 --> 00:02:00,020
One rule for this video. At a marked point
I stop and ask you for three hypotheses.

28
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,421
Pause there and write them down
before you watch the test.

29
00:02:05,261 --> 00:02:06,674
After this video you can:

30
00:02:07,014 --> 00:02:11,708
State the method: symptom, state,
evidence, hypotheses, test, root cause,

31
00:02:11,748 --> 00:02:14,432
lowest-risk fix, verification, prevention.

32
00:02:14,772 --> 00:02:18,281
Restate a colleague's report as a
symptom without interpretation.

33
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:23,580
Run the ten-command diagnosis on a repository
in an unknown state and say what each

34
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:24,603
output rules out.

35
00:02:24,943 --> 00:02:28,607
Write three hypotheses and the
read-only command that separates them.

36
00:02:28,947 --> 00:02:33,632
Explain why no state-changing command
is run before the root cause is named.

37
00:02:35,472 --> 00:02:36,968
Three facts shape the method.

38
00:02:37,308 --> 00:02:40,732
One: most damage is done by
the first repair attempt.

39
00:02:40,772 --> 00:02:45,177
Git keeps unreachable commits, the
ones no name leads to, for weeks.

40
00:02:45,217 --> 00:02:49,557
So the original problem is usually
recoverable until somebody acts.

41
00:02:49,897 --> 00:02:54,733
Two: a symptom names no layer. "CI is red"
says that the automated checks failed.

42
00:02:54,773 --> 00:02:59,609
It may be a Git fact: the commit lacks a
file. Or a GitHub fact: the check ran on

43
00:02:59,649 --> 00:03:03,643
the merge ref, which names GitHub's
test merge of the pull request.

44
00:03:03,683 --> 00:03:08,519
Or a GitHub Actions fact: the image of the
runner, the machine that runs the job,

45
00:03:08,559 --> 00:03:12,132
changed. Evidence decides, not
the wording of the complaint.

46
00:03:12,472 --> 00:03:17,945
Three: experience doesn't protect you. The
textbook cites a study of Git questions

47
00:03:17,985 --> 00:03:23,526
on Stack Exchange: in 2020, 40.0 percent
of the people asking Git-command questions

48
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:28,973
had been registered for more than five
years, against 21.2 percent of all askers.

49
00:03:29,013 --> 00:03:32,066
A fixed procedure is what
replaces confidence.

50
00:03:32,406 --> 00:03:37,448
The method, in one sentence. Diagnosis is
a read-only search for the one fact about

51
00:03:37,488 --> 00:03:42,408
the state that explains the symptom,
followed by the smallest change that repairs

52
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:43,020
that fact.

53
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:48,663
Precisely. The eleven steps of the framework
fall into three phases with different

54
00:03:48,703 --> 00:03:53,940
permissions. Phase 1 is read-only: symptom,
observe, collect evidence, understand

55
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:58,176
state, form hypotheses, test
hypotheses, identify the root cause.

56
00:03:58,216 --> 00:04:03,323
It uses only commands with the green
label. Phase 2 preserves: it adds refs and

57
00:04:03,363 --> 00:04:08,470
files and removes nothing. Phase 3 is the
first moment a ref moves or a file is

58
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:12,770
overwritten: select the lowest-risk
fix, execute, verify, prevent.

59
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:18,004
That ordering answers the fifth objective.
No state-changing command is run before

60
00:04:18,044 --> 00:04:22,938
the root cause is named, because until then
you don't know which state is evidence

61
00:04:22,978 --> 00:04:26,067
and which state is the only
copy of somebody's work.

62
00:04:26,107 --> 00:04:30,459
The one kind of change that's allowed
earlier is the kind that only adds.

63
00:04:30,799 --> 00:04:35,607
Understand state. It means being able to
fill in this table for the repository in

64
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:38,839
front of you. Each cell has
a command that answers it.

65
00:04:39,179 --> 00:04:43,784
Three of those words, in plain terms.
The working tree is the files you edit.

66
00:04:43,824 --> 00:04:48,669
The index is the proposed next commit. A
remote-tracking branch records where the

67
00:04:48,709 --> 00:04:51,021
server's branch was at your last fetch.

68
00:04:51,361 --> 00:04:56,686
Hypotheses. A hypothesis is a mechanism plus
a prediction: "if the branch was reset,

69
00:04:56,726 --> 00:05:01,923
the branch reflog has a reset: moving to
entry". The reflog is Git's local list of

70
00:05:01,963 --> 00:05:06,840
the values a ref has had. Write at least
three hypotheses before testing any.

71
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:12,077
With one hypothesis you look for confirmation.
With three you look for the command

72
00:05:12,117 --> 00:05:13,865
whose output separates them.

73
00:05:14,205 --> 00:05:18,854
Restating the report. The developer's
sentence contains an interpretation: "I added

74
00:05:18,894 --> 00:05:23,486
two commits to my pull request branch."
The symptom without interpretation is: two

75
00:05:23,526 --> 00:05:27,893
commits that the developer made aren't
shown on the pull request, and git push

76
00:05:27,933 --> 00:05:32,299
origin <branch> prints "Everything
up-to-date". Whether they were added to the

77
00:05:32,339 --> 00:05:34,277
branch is exactly what isn't known.

78
00:05:34,617 --> 00:05:39,335
In production, ask the reporter for three
things: the exact text on the screen, the

79
00:05:39,375 --> 00:05:43,406
last three commands they remember,
and whether anything was done since.

80
00:05:43,446 --> 00:05:46,616
The scrollback often contains
the commit ID you'll need.

81
00:05:48,456 --> 00:05:51,567
The textbook's analogy is
an accident investigator.

82
00:05:51,607 --> 00:05:56,570
The investigator secures the site, records
everything, reconstructs the sequence,

83
00:05:56,610 --> 00:06:01,758
and only then states a cause. Nobody tows
the vehicles away first to see whether the

84
00:06:01,798 --> 00:06:02,808
road works again.

85
00:06:03,148 --> 00:06:08,020
The analogy breaks in one useful way: a
repository can be copied in seconds, so you

86
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,927
can rehearse the repair on the
copy. No accident site offers that.

87
00:06:12,267 --> 00:06:15,235
Carry one more picture for
"Everything up-to-date".

88
00:06:15,275 --> 00:06:19,954
It's a statement about one ref: the local
branch and the server's branch of that

89
00:06:19,994 --> 00:06:23,965
name point at the same commit. It
isn't a statement about your work.

90
00:06:24,005 --> 00:06:28,801
A push sends a ref. If the ref didn't move,
there's nothing to send, wherever your

91
00:06:28,841 --> 00:06:29,509
commits are.

92
00:06:31,349 --> 00:06:36,010
The three phases on one sheet. The demo
stays in the left column until the root

93
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:37,021
cause has a name.

94
00:06:37,361 --> 00:06:42,422
The state diagram comes later, after the
evidence has been collected, and so does

95
00:06:42,462 --> 00:06:47,586
the root-cause box. Before the demo, only
the first line of that box can be filled

96
00:06:47,626 --> 00:06:50,923
in: the observed behavior.
That's all we know so far.

97
00:06:52,763 --> 00:06:53,408
The symptom.

98
00:06:53,748 --> 00:06:58,194
"Everything up-to-date": the local and the
server's feature/latency-budget name the

99
00:06:58,234 --> 00:07:01,706
same commit. It says nothing about
where the two new commits are.

100
00:07:02,046 --> 00:07:06,544
Command 1: git status. Look at the
first line before anything else.

101
00:07:06,584 --> 00:07:11,828
It names the current branch, or says that
HEAD is detached, meaning it holds a

102
00:07:11,868 --> 00:07:16,569
commit ID instead of a branch name,
or names an operation in progress.

103
00:07:16,609 --> 00:07:19,279
Predict the first line. Say it out loud.

104
00:07:24,119 --> 00:07:29,392
The first line isn't "On branch". A rebase
of feature/latency-budget onto 4b30d7e

105
00:07:29,432 --> 00:07:33,590
was started and never finished:
one command done, two remaining.

106
00:07:33,630 --> 00:07:37,657
The working tree is clean, so
nothing uncommitted is at stake.

107
00:07:37,997 --> 00:07:42,723
Command 2: git branch -vv. Look for the
star, for the upstream in brackets, and for

108
00:07:42,763 --> 00:07:47,317
ahead, behind and gone. The upstream is
the server branch that a local branch is

109
00:07:47,357 --> 00:07:48,121
compared with.

110
00:07:48,461 --> 00:07:54,789
The star is on "no branch, rebasing". The
branch feature/latency-budget still points

111
00:07:54,829 --> 00:08:01,157
at 4a03014, with no ahead: it equals its
upstream. HEAD is on 075407e, a commit that

112
00:08:01,197 --> 00:08:02,370
no branch names.

113
00:08:02,710 --> 00:08:06,856
Command 3: git remote -v. A remote
is a name for another repository.

114
00:08:06,896 --> 00:08:11,227
Look for more than one remote, and
for fetch and push URLs that differ.

115
00:08:11,567 --> 00:08:16,102
One remote, one URL. That rules
out a push to another repository.

116
00:08:16,442 --> 00:08:21,479
Command 4: the graph. Look for where each
name sits, and for commits with no name

117
00:08:21,519 --> 00:08:22,231
beside them.

118
00:08:22,571 --> 00:08:28,392
Two lines of history leave 8bd6e7d. The
lower one is the published branch, ending at

119
00:08:28,432 --> 00:08:28,950
4a03014.

120
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:34,654
The upper one starts at 4b30d7e on main
and holds a second "Add latency budget to

121
00:08:34,694 --> 00:08:39,058
config", followed by the two commits
the developer is looking for.

122
00:08:39,098 --> 00:08:40,592
Only HEAD decorates it.

123
00:08:40,932 --> 00:08:46,010
Command 5: git reflog. Read it from the
bottom up: it's the sequence of events.

124
00:08:46,350 --> 00:08:52,069
HEAD@{3} is "rebase (start): checkout main".
There's no "rebase (finish)" line above

125
00:08:52,109 --> 00:08:57,828
it. The three entries above the start are
plain commit entries: one for the conflict

126
00:08:57,868 --> 00:08:59,817
resolution, two for new work.

127
00:09:00,157 --> 00:09:05,494
Command 6: git rev-parse. Turn names into
exact IDs, and ask whether HEAD is a

128
00:09:05,534 --> 00:09:05,977
branch.

129
00:09:06,317 --> 00:09:11,158
The local branch and its remote-tracking
branch are the same object, which is why

130
00:09:11,198 --> 00:09:16,100
the push had nothing to send. git symbolic-ref
HEAD fails: HEAD holds a commit ID,

131
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:17,606
not the name of a branch.

132
00:09:17,946 --> 00:09:23,185
Command 7: git show --stat HEAD. Is
this the commit the reporter means?

133
00:09:23,525 --> 00:09:28,804
Commands 8 and 9: git diff and git diff
--cached. Look for work that exists in no

134
00:09:28,844 --> 00:09:33,007
commit. It's the work that a wrong
fix destroys without a trace.

135
00:09:33,347 --> 00:09:38,513
Both empty. Everything of value here is
committed. Remember this output: one later

136
00:09:38,553 --> 00:09:40,736
step is allowed only because of it.

137
00:09:41,076 --> 00:09:44,438
Command 10: the configuration,
with origin and scope.

138
00:09:44,478 --> 00:09:49,765
Look for the branch.<name>.remote and
branch.<name>.merge pairs, and for pull, push

139
00:09:49,805 --> 00:09:51,049
and rebase settings.

140
00:09:51,389 --> 00:09:56,535
The upstream of feature/latency-budget is
the branch of the same name on origin.

141
00:09:56,575 --> 00:10:01,851
No setting changes the behavior of push or
rebase. The two gc.reflog lines are the

142
00:10:01,891 --> 00:10:03,018
lab configuration.

143
00:10:03,358 --> 00:10:07,675
And git ls-files. Are the files the
reporter talks about tracked at the current

144
00:10:07,715 --> 00:10:08,061
commit?

145
00:10:08,401 --> 00:10:14,984
app/metrics.py and app/audit.py, the files
of the two new commits, are in the index.

146
00:10:15,024 --> 00:10:16,246
The work exists.

147
00:10:16,586 --> 00:10:21,725
Here's that state as one picture. The
lower line in the clone equals the server.

148
00:10:21,765 --> 00:10:25,868
The upper line exists only in the
clone, and only HEAD names it.

149
00:10:26,208 --> 00:10:29,639
The state files. A rebase keeps
its state in a directory.

150
00:10:32,979 --> 00:10:36,326
head-name is the branch that will
be moved when the rebase ends.

151
00:10:36,366 --> 00:10:40,031
orig-head is where it was when the
rebase began. onto is the new base.

152
00:10:40,071 --> 00:10:42,995
done and git-rebase-todo are
the two halves of the plan.

153
00:10:43,335 --> 00:10:47,543
Stop the video here. You have the
symptom and every piece of evidence.

154
00:10:47,583 --> 00:10:52,580
Write three hypotheses, each as a mechanism
plus a prediction, and for each the one

155
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:56,100
read-only command whose output
would confirm or reject it.

156
00:10:56,140 --> 00:10:58,831
Don't continue until you
have three on paper.

157
00:11:03,671 --> 00:11:08,222
The test. The textbook's four hypotheses.
One: the commits were made on another

158
00:11:08,262 --> 00:11:12,696
branch. Two: a rebase stopped and the
commits were made on the detached HEAD.

159
00:11:12,736 --> 00:11:16,532
Three: the commits were pushed and
the pull request page is stale.

160
00:11:16,572 --> 00:11:18,972
Four: the push went to another repository.

161
00:11:19,312 --> 00:11:24,044
The first command lists what HEAD has
that the branch doesn't: the two missing

162
00:11:24,084 --> 00:11:27,532
commits, the conflict resolution,
and the commit of main.

163
00:11:27,572 --> 00:11:31,753
The server holds 4a03014 for the
branch, so nothing newer was pushed.

164
00:11:31,793 --> 00:11:36,770
And --contains HEAD lists only the detached
HEAD: no other branch has the commits.

165
00:11:37,110 --> 00:11:40,109
One hypothesis is confirmed
and three are rejected.

166
00:11:40,449 --> 00:11:43,662
That's the root-cause box,
filled in. The layer is Git.

167
00:11:43,702 --> 00:11:48,395
GitHub displayed the branch it was given.
And there are the two commits from the

168
00:11:48,435 --> 00:11:51,708
opening: on a detached HEAD,
where no branch names them.

169
00:11:52,048 --> 00:11:55,974
Preserve. Before any change,
give the unnamed commits a name.

170
00:11:56,014 --> 00:11:59,680
🟢 SAFE: git branch adds one
ref and touches nothing else.

171
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:01,634
Select the lowest-risk fix.

172
00:12:01,974 --> 00:12:07,167
Option C doesn't bring main into the
branch. That's a separate decision, and

173
00:12:07,207 --> 00:12:11,023
rescue/latency-wip keeps the
conflict resolution for it.

174
00:12:11,363 --> 00:12:16,750
Execute, one change at a time. 🟡 CAUTION:
git rebase --abort resets the index and

175
00:12:16,790 --> 00:12:22,310
the working tree to the original branch
tip, so it's run only because outputs 8 and

176
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:23,181
9 were empty.

177
00:12:23,521 --> 00:12:27,952
"On branch" again. 🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick
adds commits to the current branch.

178
00:12:34,292 --> 00:12:40,693
The picks created c54eb8c and 30e8fe4: the
same changes as 96a7b55 and 075407e with

179
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:44,419
a different parent, and
therefore different IDs.

180
00:12:44,759 --> 00:12:50,194
Preview the push, then push. Quick quiz:
what will the preview line look like for a

181
00:12:50,234 --> 00:12:53,953
fast-forward, where the server's
branch only moves ahead?

182
00:12:53,993 --> 00:12:57,845
A, two dots between the IDs.
B, a plus sign and three dots.

183
00:12:57,885 --> 00:12:58,637
Your answer?

184
00:13:03,477 --> 00:13:08,368
A. Two dots: a fast-forward. A forced update
would print a plus sign and three dots.

185
00:13:08,708 --> 00:13:13,306
Verify. Run the commands that showed the
problem and check that each output changed

186
00:13:13,346 --> 00:13:14,703
for the predicted reason.

187
00:13:15,043 --> 00:13:20,433
The first line of git status is "On branch".
HEAD, the remote-tracking branch and

188
00:13:20,473 --> 00:13:24,255
the server agree on 30e8fe4.
No rebase directory remains.

189
00:13:24,595 --> 00:13:29,093
git cherry -v compares by patch, that
is, by the change and not by the ID.

190
00:13:29,133 --> 00:13:34,183
The minus sign marks the two rescued commits
as present on the branch in equivalent

191
00:13:34,223 --> 00:13:39,150
form. The plus sign marks the commit of
main and the conflict resolution 9fbe4d7,

192
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:41,174
which were deliberately left out.

193
00:13:41,514 --> 00:13:45,779
Prevent. Make a stopped operation
visible where people look.

194
00:13:45,819 --> 00:13:51,806
Git ships a prompt script, git-prompt.sh,
whose __git_ps1 function prints the branch

195
00:13:51,846 --> 00:13:57,690
and the operation in progress. The habit
that needs no tool: the first line of git

196
00:13:57,730 --> 00:14:00,704
status before every commit and every push.

197
00:14:01,044 --> 00:14:06,626
Try it now. Thirty seconds. In the lab shell,
or in any repository you have, run git

198
00:14:06,666 --> 00:14:10,508
status, which only reads, and
say its first line out loud.

199
00:14:15,348 --> 00:14:20,397
That line named your branch, or said that
HEAD is detached, or named an operation in

200
00:14:20,437 --> 00:14:24,698
progress. Whichever it was, you knew
it before you typed anything else.

201
00:14:24,738 --> 00:14:26,091
That's the whole habit.

202
00:14:27,931 --> 00:14:29,580
Five mistakes to watch for.

203
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:34,304
Acting on the reporter's interpretation.
Root cause: "I added two commits to my

204
00:14:34,344 --> 00:14:39,008
branch" is a belief about the state, and
the state is what has not been established.

205
00:14:39,348 --> 00:14:42,873
Reading "Everything up-to-date"
as "my work is on the server".

206
00:14:42,913 --> 00:14:47,646
Root cause: the message compares one local
ref with one server ref and says nothing

207
00:14:47,686 --> 00:14:49,487
about commits that no ref names.

208
00:14:49,827 --> 00:14:54,658
Testing the first hypothesis that comes to
mind. Root cause: with one hypothesis you

209
00:14:54,698 --> 00:14:59,298
look for confirmation; only several
hypotheses make you look for the output that

210
00:14:59,338 --> 00:15:00,168
separates them.

211
00:15:00,508 --> 00:15:05,434
Aborting the rebase first. Root cause: the
abort returns HEAD to the original branch

212
